Text: ZURICH (Reuters) - Switzerland has stripped a Libyan Muslim preacher of his refugee status nearly two decades after he won it following a media firestorm over his sermons that some have branded hate speech. The man who goes by the name of Abu Ramadan has been in the headlines since a Zurich newspaper branded him a Islamist who incited hate in his sermons while living off more than 600,000 Swiss francs ($618,000) in social benefits. The 64-year-old preacher denied the hate speech allegations, saying his remarks were poorly translated from Arabic. In a ruling released on Friday, the Federal Administrative Court said immigration officials had been right to revoke Ramadan s asylum after finding he had kept a Libyan passport and gone home a dozen times, most recently for more than a month this year. The ruling is final and cannot be appealed but separate hearings will have to decide if the man can stay in Switzerland. The preacher has told Swiss media he was unaware he could not travel to his homeland as a refugee and wanted to see his 93-year-old mother.
Answer:

generated_pred:
{
  "Ethical_Stabilizers": {
    "is_used": "Yes",
    "explanation": "The Federal Administrative Court and immigration officials are framed as authoritative actors upholding rules and justice: the piece states the court 'said immigration officials had been right to revoke Ramadan's asylum' and notes the ruling is final, portraying institutions as enforcing legal standards."
  },
  "Altruistic_Catalysts": {
    "is_used": "No",
    "explanation": "The text does not portray anyone as challenging the status quo at personal cost or as a sacrificial/underdog reformer; it focuses on legal and accusatory dimensions rather than activism or martyrdom."
  },
  "Overt_Aggressors": {
    "is_used": "Yes",
    "explanation": "The preacher is framed as an initiator of harm through accusations of hate speech: he was 'branded...an Islamist who incited hate' and his 'sermons' are described as having been labeled 'hate speech,' which casts him in an instigator/agitator role."
  },
  "Deceptive_Subversives": {
    "is_used": "No",
    "explanation": "Although the ruling cites that he 'kept a Libyan passport and gone home a dozen times,' the text presents this as factual grounds for revocation rather than emphasizing covert conspiracy, espionage, or systematic deception; therefore it does not clearly frame a conspiratorial subversive role."
  },
  "Institutional_Toxins": {
    "is_used": "No",
    "explanation": "Institutions (the court, immigration officials, media) are not depicted as corrupt or incompetent; the court's decision is presented as legitimate and the media's role is described factually as having 'branded' him, so there is no strong framing of institutional failure."
  },
  "Marginalized_Sufferers": {
    "is_used": "No",
    "explanation": "While the subject is a refugee and mentions seeing his 93‑year‑old mother, the narrative centers on accusations and legal revocation rather than emphasizing passive victimhood, exploitation, or systemic neglect."
  }
}

